meter name=\"$PARAMETER_NAME\">$PARAMETER_VALUE</atem:parameter>\n...\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls>\n\nString and scalar parameters should be specified as is, while lists and objects should use JSON format. Note that spaces for string values are not stripped. The output is not expected to be valid XML and is parsed with regular expressions.\nHere are the functions available in JSONSchema format:\n// Tool metadata\n{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"list_dir\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"finish\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"run_background_command\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"run_command_and_capture_output\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"list_background_commands\", \"description\": \"\"}\n{\"name\": \"stop_background_command\", \"description\": \"\"}\n// Function schemas\n{\"name\": \"read_file\", \"description\": \"Read a file in the workspace.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"write_file\", \"description\": \"Create or overwrite a file. Any missing parent directories in `path` are created automatically.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"content\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"content\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"edit_file\", \"description\": \"Replace a unique substring in a file.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"old_str\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"new_str\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"path\", \"old_str\", \"new_str\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"list_dir\", \"description\": \"List a directory in the workspace.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"path\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": []}}\n{\"name\": \"finish\", \"description\": \"Signal that the task is complete.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"summary\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": []}}\n{\"name\": \"run_command\", \"description\": \"Run a shell command in the workspace and see its stdout/stderr.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"command\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"command\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"run_background_command\", \"description\": \"Run a shell command in the workspace in the background and return its PID. Useful for long-running processes like dev servers.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"command\": {\"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"command\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"run_command_and_capture_output\", \"description\": \"Run a command for a maximum duration and return its captured output. Useful for commands where you just want to sample output for a few seconds. NOT for starting servers - use run_background_command for those.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"command\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"duration\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}}, \"required\": [\"command\", \"duration\"]}}\n{\"name\": \"list_background_commands\", \"description\": \"List background processes you started in this run, with their PIDs and the ports they are listening on.\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {}, \"required\": []}}\n{\"name\": \"stop_background_command\", \"description\": \"Stop a background process you started earlier (e.g. to free its port before restarting a server).\", \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\"pid\": {\"type\": \"integer\"}}, \"required\": [\"pid\"]}}\n\nHere's an example of how to call a function in the tool set:\n(If the tool namespace is not specified, invoke the function directly as `example_function_name` rather than `example_tool_name.example_function_name`)\n\nto=example_tool_name.example_function_name\n\n<atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"example_tool_name.example_function_name\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"example_parameter_1\">value_1</atem:parameter>\n<atem:parameter name=\"example_parameter_2\">This is the value for the second parameter\nthat can span\n\"multiple\" lines\n</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls>\n\n# Valid recipients: \"self\", \"read_file.*\", \"write_file.*\", \"edit_file.*\", \"list_dir.*\", \"finish.*\", \"run_command.*\", \"run_background_command.*\", \"run_command_and_capture_output.*\", \"list_background_commands.*\", \"stop_background_command.*\", \"user\".<|eot|><|start|>user<|message|>## Context\n\nWe are building a traditional client-server web application with real-time capabilities. To minimize external dependencies while retaining the power of PostgreSQL, we are using PGLite embedded within a Node.js server. Real-time updates will be pushed to the client using Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- Provide a simple message board where users can post text messages.\n- Ensure all connected clients receive updates instantly when new messages are posted.\n- Store data persistently on the local disk using PGLite.\n- Keep the frontend incredibly simple and lightweight.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- User authentication and authorization.\n- Rich text formatting or media attachments.\n- Horizontal scaling across multiple server instances (PGLite is tied to the single Node instance).\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Decision 1: Embedded PGLite vs External Postgres**\n- *Rationale*: PGLite allows us to run PostgreSQL directly inside the Node.js process. This means users don't need to install or configure a separate database service. It seamlessly handles raw SQL queries and can persist to the filesystem.\n\n**Decision 2: Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets**\n- *Rationale*: We only need one-way real-time communication (Server pushing new messages to Clients). Clients can post new messages via standard HTTP POST requests. SSE is much simpler to implement, works natively over standard HTTP, and doesn't require a complex WebSocket library like Socket.io.\n\n**Decision 3: Vanilla JS / Vite vs React**\n- *Rationale*: Given the minimal UI requirements (a message list and a text input), Vanilla JS keeps the build extremely fast and the dependency tree small. Vite provides a great developer experience out of the box.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Risk] PGLite performance on very large datasets: Embedded WASM databases are incredibly fast but might struggle if the dataset grows to tens of gigabytes.\n  - *Mitigation*: This is a local application and we expect message volume to be manageable for a single server instance.\n- [Risk] SSE connection limits: Browsers have limits on the number of concurrent SSE connections per domain.\n  - *Mitigation*: We only need one SSE connection per client tab, which is well within browser limits.\n\n## Why\n\nWe need a fast, local-first collaborative message board that allows users to post messages and see updates in real-time. This provides an incredibly low-latency experience by keeping data local while ensuring all users see the same state instantly. It serves as an excellent demonstration of using embedded PGLite combined with Server-Sent Events (SSE) to create a robust, lightweight realtime application without external database dependencies.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- We are introducing a brand new traditional client-server web application.\n- The backend will be a Node.js server using an embedded PGLite database for raw SQL queries, persisting data to the local disk.\n- Real-time synchronization will be handled via Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n- The frontend will be a simple, lightweight Single Page Application (Vanilla JS or React via Vite).\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `realtime-board`: The core message board capability covering posting messages, viewing a message feed, and receiving live updates via SSE.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\nNone (Greenfield project).\n\n## Impact\n\nThis is a brand new project. It will establish the foundational architecture including the Node.js backend setup, the embedded PGLite database structure, the SSE connection handling, and the frontend web UI.\n\n## 1. Project Initialization\n\n- [ ] 1.1 Initialize the Node.js project and install backend dependencies (express, @electric-sql/pglite, cors)\n- [ ] 1.2 Initialize the Vanilla JS / Vite project for the frontend and install frontend dependencies\n- [ ] 1.3 Set up basic npm scripts for concurrently running backend and frontend development servers\n- [ ] 1.4 Make the app start from a fresh checkout with no manual steps: once the dependencies declared in its package.json files are installed, `npm run dev` at the project root starts both the backend and the frontend, the backend creates any directory it needs (such as PGLite's data directory), and the page the frontend serves reaches the backend's API and event stream\n\n## 2. Backend Infrastructure & Database\n\n- [ ] 2.1 Set up a basic Express server with CORS and JSON body parsing\n- [ ] 2.2 Initialize embedded PGLite configured to write to the local file system\n- [ ] 2.3 Create the database initialization script (create `messages` table with `id`, `text`, `created_at`)\n- [ ] 2.4 Create the historical messages endpoint (`GET /api/messages`) to fetch initial state\n\n## 3. Real-time Capabilities (SSE)\n\n- [ ] 3.1 Implement the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) that maintains active connections with clients\n- [ ] 3.2 Implement the messaging endpoint (`POST /api/messages`) to insert text into PGLite\n- [ ] 3.3 Broadcast new messages to all active SSE connections when a new message is successfully inserted\n\n## 4. Frontend Implementation\n\n- [ ] 4.1 Create the basic HTML layout containing a message list and a text input form\n- [ ] 4.2 Fetch the message history (`GET /api/messages`) on initial page load and render messages\n- [ ] 4.3 Connect to the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) using `EventSource` to listen for new messages\n- [ ] 4.4 Append incoming real-time messages to the DOM seamlessly\n- [ ] 4.5 Wire up the form submission to send a `POST` request to `/api/messages` and clear the input\n\n<|eot|><|start|>assistant to=list_dir<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"list_dir\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"path\">/</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool list_dir<|message|><tool_output name=\"list_dir\">\nERROR bad arguments: path escapes workspace: /\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant to=list_dir<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"list_dir\">\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool list_dir<|message|><tool_output name=\"list_dir\">\n(empty)\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant to=write_file<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"write_file\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"path\">/tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/package.json</atem:parameter>\n<atem:parameter name=\"content\">{\n  \"name\": \"message-board\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"concurrently \\\"npm run backend:dev\\\" \\\"npm run frontend:dev\\\"\",\n    \"backend:dev\": \"cd backend && node server.js\",\n    \"frontend:dev\": \"cd frontend && npm run dev\",\n    \"install-all\": \"npm install && cd backend && npm install && cd ../frontend && npm install\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"concurrently\": \"^8.2.2\"\n  }\n}\n</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool write_file<|message|><tool_output name=\"write_file\">\nwrote 420 chars to /tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/package.json\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant to=run_command<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"run_command\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"command\">mkdir -p backend frontend</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool run_command<|message|><tool_output name=\"run_command\">\n$ mkdir -p backend frontend\n[exit 0]\n\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant to=write_file<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"write_file\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"path\">/tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/backend/package.json</atem:parameter>\n<atem:parameter name=\"content\">{\n  \"name\": \"backend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"main\": \"server.js\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"start\": \"node server.js\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"express\": \"^4.19.2\",\n    \"@electric-sql/pglite\": \"^0.2.0\",\n    \"cors\": \"^2.8.5\"\n  }\n}\n</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool write_file<|message|><tool_output name=\"write_file\">\nwrote 249 chars to /tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/backend/package.json\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant to=write_file<|message|><atem:function_calls>\n<atem:invoke name=\"write_file\">\n<atem:parameter name=\"path\">/tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/backend/server.js</atem:parameter>\n<atem:parameter name=\"content\">import express from 'express';\nimport cors from 'cors';\nimport { PGlite } from '@electric-sql/pglite';\nimport { join, dirname } from 'path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'url';\nimport { mkdir } from 'fs/promises';\n\nconst __filename = fileURLToPath(import.meta.url);\nconst __dirname = dirname(__filename);\n\nconst app = express();\nconst PORT = 3001;\n\napp.use(cors());\napp.use(express.json());\n\n// Initialize PGLite with file system persistence\nconst dataDir = join(__dirname, 'data');\nawait mkdir(dataDir, { recursive: true });\n\nconst pg = new PGlite(join(dataDir, 'db'));\n\n// Initialize database\nawait pg.exec(`\n  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS messages (\n    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n    text TEXT NOT NULL,\n    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ DEFAULT NOW()\n  )\n`);\n\n// Track SSE clients\nconst clients = new Set();\n\nfunction broadcastMessage(message) {\n  const data = `data: ${JSON.stringify(message)}\\n\\n`;\n  for (const client of clients) {\n    client.write(data);\n  }\n}\n\n// SSE endpoint\napp.get('/api/stream', (req, res) => {\n  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/event-stream');\n  res.setHeader('Cache-Control', 'no-cache');\n  res.setHeader('Connection', 'keep-alive');\n  res.setHeader('X-Accel-Buffering', 'no');\n  \n  res.write('\\n'); // Initial newline\n  \n  clients.add(res);\n  \n  req.on('close', () => {\n    clients.delete(res);\n  });\n});\n\n// Get messages\napp.get('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  try {\n    const result = await pg.query('SELECT id, text, created_at FROM messages ORDER BY created_at ASC');\n    res.json(result.rows);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(err);\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to fetch messages' });\n  }\n});\n\n// Post message\napp.post('/api/messages', async (req, res) => {\n  try {\n    const { text } = req.body;\n    if (!text || typeof text !== 'string' || text.trim() === '') {\n      return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Message text is required' });\n    }\n    \n    const result = await pg.query(\n      'INSERT INTO messages (text) VALUES ($1) RETURNING id, text, created_at',\n      [text.trim()]\n    );\n    \n    const message = result.rows[0];\n    broadcastMessage(message);\n    \n    res.status(201).json(message);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.error(err);\n    res.status(500).json({ error: 'Failed to save message' });\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(PORT, () => {\n  console.log(`Backend server running on http://localhost:${PORT}`);\n});\n</atem:parameter>\n</atem:invoke>\n</atem:function_calls><|eot|><|start|>tool write_file<|message|><tool_output name=\"write_file\">\nwrote 2371 chars to /tmp/runs/smokes/muse-q4xl/message-board/execution/runs/201/app/backend/server.js\n</tool_output><|eot|><|start|>assistant","generated":""}
